--TEST--
A `Core\Task::all` deadline stops a child that never waits, because the child gives its core back after every time slice and the deadline's timer fires there; the request's `cpu_time` is far away and is not what stops it — `rule:concurrency/limit-and-deadline-are-the-only-bounds`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Far longer than the deadline, so only the deadline can end the call in time.
[limits]
cpu_time = "10s"
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/limit-and-deadline
class Spins {
    public static int $count = 0;
}

try {
    Core\Task::all({
        spin: fn(): int => {
            while (true) {
                Spins::$count += 1;
            }
            return Spins::$count;
        },
    }, {deadline: 50ms});
    echo "the call returned", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "the deadline stopped the child", "\n";
}

// The child was torn down before the call returned, so nothing still counts.
int $before = Spins::$count;
Core\Time::sleep(20ms);
echo Spins::$count == $before ? "the child is gone" : "the child still runs", "\n";
--EXPECT--
the deadline stopped the child
the child is gone
